    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d396-a1">

                <pb facs="0810" n="796" xml:id="pg796"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure No. 1.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Barlow, United
                        States marshal, to Mr. Hoar,
                        Attorney-General.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <dateline><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">New York City</hi></placeName>,
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1869-06-29"><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >June</hi> 29, 1869</date>.</dateline>
                <p rendition="#flush">To the <hi rendition="#small-caps">Attorney-General</hi>:</p>
                <p>Three tugs, two schooners, and a sloop left Gardner’s Point this morning at 5
                    a.m. They had been notified of the seizure of the Whiting.</p>
                <p><seg type="margin">[76]</seg> It is impossible that they should attempt to go to
                    Cuba in their present vessels; *probably they will run down the coast to some
                    port to wait for another steamer. I think the cutters at Philadelphia, and at
                    all points on the coast, should be ordered to put out and look for them. Also
                    the Newport and New Bedford authorities should be notified. They will try to get
                    a steamer at some other port. I should like to be informed of what orders may be
                    given.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>FRANCIS. C. BARLOW.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
